WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.571
A rule applies only to the subject, population and circumstances it states.

2
00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:08.281
A connection must preserve those restrictions.

3
00:00:08.581 --> 00:00:11.141
Every sample in the export register is sealed.

4
00:00:11.441 --> 00:00:14.449
Every sample in the research register is retained.

5
00:00:14.749 --> 00:00:17.992
Sample B is in the export register, so it is sealed.

6
00:00:18.292 --> 00:00:20.788
That does not put B in the research register.

7
00:00:21.088 --> 00:00:26.208
A short label such as registered would conceal the difference and manufacture a connection.

8
00:00:26.508 --> 00:00:32.076
Both registers may overlap, but the premises have not said that this sample belongs to both.

9
00:00:32.376 --> 00:00:39.864
Many difficult conditional errors preserve arrow direction while quietly changing a noun, qualification or reference.

10
00:00:40.164 --> 00:00:42.681
Matching the whole predicate blocks those errors.

11
00:00:42.981 --> 00:00:47.312
Retain the site, time, type of approval and person who has the property.

12
00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:51.175
Condense only after those features remain recognizable.

13
00:00:51.475 --> 00:00:55.827
When a conclusion feels too easy, compare the complete labels at its join.

14
00:00:56.127 --> 00:01:00.393
Ask whose property it is, which group is covered and which time is relevant.

15
00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:05.451
A missing bridge is missing information, not permission to shorten the labels further.
